\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] in analysis, no test is 
performed on the attribute values of the VERBP, which may not be default due to 
the actions performed by the surface parser; in generation however, these 
values are not retrieved (they are not needed for `leaves'), but simply remain 
default.
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
It is supposed that the identificational NPVAR/CNVAR does not assign anything 
to its NPPROP but aktionsarts. The 
condition on T2 has been added to distinguish the right model in this rule from 
the right model in the ordinary NPClauseFormation rule (of course, the 
attribute values of the NPPROP also differ). Note that the condition assumes 
that in the identificational subgrammar only NP/CNVARs are introduced, never 
full NPs. Finally, it is assumed that for English no 
special idrel need exist outside the identificational subgrammar; any `object'
will behave the same, whether identificational or not.
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
