\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% RULE RPreppClauseFormation
< m1: PREPPPROP{PREPPPROPrec1}
               [ mu1,
                 predrel/I1::PREPP{PREPPrec1}[mu2],
                 mu3
               ] 
>

< m : I2::CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
                [ mu1,
                  predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                         [head/VERB{VERBrec1}
                              [head/SUBVERB{SUBVERBrec1}
                                   [head/BVERB(AUX_beBVERBkey){BVERBrec1}]
                              ],
                          predrel/I3::PREPP{PREPPrec1}[mu2]
                         ],
                  mu3
                ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: PREPPrec1.actsubcefs * [loc, dir, other] <> []
       I2: synBE IN CLAUSErec1.synvpefs
       I3: PREPPrec1.actsubcefs * [other] <> []
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: PREPPPROPrec1 = COPYT_preppTOpreppprop(PREPPrec1)
                EXCEPT FOR [aktionsarts, superdeixis, 
                            PROsubject]
          A1: CLAUSErec1 := { voice       : active
                              thetavp     : thetavpaux
                              synvpefs    : [synBE]
                              aktionsarts : PREPPPROPrec1.aktionsarts
                              superdeixis : PREPPPROPrec1.superdeixis
                              PROsubject  : PREPPPROPrec1.PROsubject};
              SUBVERBrec1 := COPYT_bverbTOsubverb(BVERBrec1);
              VERBrec1 := COPYT_subverbTOverb(SUBVERBrec1)
              (* rest of attributes gets default values *)
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: CLAUSErec1 = CLAUSErecdef EXCEPT FOR [voice, thetavp, synvpefs,
                 aktionsarts, superdeixis, PROsubject]
              AND
              CLAUSErec1 = 
                           {
                              voice       :  active 
                              thetavp     :  thetavpaux
                           }  AND
              SUBVERBrec1 = COPYT_bverbTOsubverb(BVERBrec1) AND
              SUBVERBrec1.affix = noaffix AND
              VERBrec1 = COPYT_subverbTOverb(SUBVERBrec1) AND
              VERBrec1 = { numbers   : []
                           persons   : []
                           modus     : omegamodus
                           tense     : omegatense }
          A1: PREPPPROPrec1 := COPYT_preppTOpreppprop(PREPPrec1);
              PREPPPROPrec1 := { aktionsarts : CLAUSErec1.aktionsarts
                                 superdeixis : CLAUSErec1.superdeixis
                                 PROsubject  : CLAUSErec1.PROsubject
                               }
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RPreppClauseFormation
\item[* relevant examples:] x1 against the proposal $\rightarrow$ x1 be against 
the proposal
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_
ClauseFormation
\item[* date of creation:] 8-APR-1988 16:58:42  
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch RClauseFormation7
\item[* modifications:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 8/11/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted rule to admit only [other] preps in analysis (loc and dir preps had
been excluded before, but approx and topic etc were not excluded), and to admit 
loc and dir preps in generation, so that {\em de jongen in de tuin } will also 
have a paraphrase with a relative sentence {\em the boy who is in the garden }.
In generation, approx and topic etc are not allowed.
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to make a clause with a real VERBP and a head verb out of a 
PREPPPROP. Only for non-locative and non-directional PREPPs: 
the verb `be' in 
`He is in the garden' is a different `be': a basic expression taking a locative 
argument, to be translated in the Dutch `staan/liggen/zitten/zijn' etc.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] in analysis, no test is 
performed on the attribute values of the VERBP, which may not be default due to 
the actions performed by the surface parser; in generation however, these 
values are not retrieved (they are not needed for `leaves'), but simply remain 
default.\\
In generation, it is allowed to introduce the verb {\em be } for dir and loc 
preps, in order to allow for a relative sentence. Note that {\em The girl who 
is in the garden } will never translate to {\em Het meisje in de tuin }, since 
{\em be } is considered a main verb. The other way around does occur, however.
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
It is assumed that the PREPPPROP indeed gets its attribute values through a 
copy function from the PREPP. 
\end{enumerate}
